clautolisp-windows-pathname-mapping — a path/pathname mapping layer for cross-environment Windows
=================================================================================================

Reported from: pathname-resolution problems surfaced while loading
cadaptor under clautolisp (relative paths, cwd/PWD), generalised to
the underlying Windows portability problem.
Observed on: design issue; concrete symptoms on clautolisp 1.2.7 /
alfe 1.1.16. Primary target: MS-Windows builds/runs.

Terminology
-----------

- pathname : the Common Lisp notion (structured object).
- path     : the POSIX / AutoCAD / BricsCAD notion (a plain string).

The AutoLISP/AutoCAD/BricsCAD API works with PATH STRINGS, so
clautolisp must be compatible with them; it cannot expose CL
pathnames to user code.

Summary
-------

There is a fundamental problem mapping CL pathnames to physical paths.

On macOS and Linux, CL implementations are well-behaved: the mapping
from pathnames to paths is handled correctly, roots are unambiguous
("/"), and a single environment is in play.

On MS-Windows there is NOT a single environment. Several coexisting
environments use different path syntaxes and different virtual mount
points / roots for the same physical files:

- Native Windows: drive-letter roots, e.g. `C:/...`.
- Cygwin / MSYS2: POSIX-style roots; `C:/` appears as `/c/` or
  `/mnt/c` (the latter under WSL).
- From native Windows, an MSYS2 `/` is actually something like
  `C:/msys64/`.
- `~/` (home) resolves to a variety of paths depending on the
  environment.

Worse:

- A program such as SBCL may have been COMPILED in one environment
  (msys2, WSL, native) and RUN in another. WSL is especially nasty
  because it presents itself as Linux (`uname` says Linux) while the
  surrounding Windows paths still exist.
- The environment clautolisp was COMPILED in can differ from the
  environment it is RUN in, which can differ again from the syntax of
  the paths the USER passes to it.

So at runtime there are (at least) three frames of reference to
reconcile:

1. what the CL implementation expects as physical pathnames — syntax
   and roots / mount points — i.e. essentially the environment SBCL
   (or the host CL) was compiled in;
2. what the run-time environment expects (which Windows environment we
   are actually running under);
3. the paths the user gives us (in yet another syntax/rooting).

Expected
--------

clautolisp needs a path-mapping layer (conceptually similar to
logical pathnames) that:

- knows frame (1): the physical-pathname syntax and roots the
  underlying CL implementation was built for;
- knows frame (2): the path syntax and mount points of the runtime
  environment;
- can transform / map paths between (3) the user-supplied syntax and
  whatever the CL runtime actually needs to hit the right physical
  file.

Constraints / notes
-------------------

- We cannot "just use CL logical pathnames": the AutoLISP/AutoCAD/
  BricsCAD API passes plain path strings, and user code expects path
  strings back. The mapping must be compatible with that string-based
  API and be transparent to user code.
- Exposing CL logical pathnames as a *clautolisp extension* (opt-in,
  on top of the string API) is a separate idea worth considering, but
  it does not replace the mapping layer above.
- macOS/Linux can keep the trivial identity mapping; the layer only
  needs to do real work on Windows / cross-environment builds.

Why it matters
--------------

Without this, clautolisp on Windows will mis-resolve user paths
whenever the compile-time and run-time environments disagree (a very
common situation: SBCL from msys2/WSL, run under native Windows or
vice-versa). Path strings that work in one frame silently fail or hit
the wrong file in another.

Suggested direction
-------------------

- Detect, at build time, the physical-pathname syntax/roots the host
  CL expects (record it in the image).
- Detect, at run time, the active Windows environment (native /
  Cygwin / MSYS2 / WSL) and its mount-point mapping.
- Implement a canonicalisation/translation step applied to every path
  string crossing the API boundary (in: user/AutoLISP -> CL; out: CL
  -> user/AutoLISP), driven by the (build-env, run-env) pair.
- Add a conformance test matrix across the environment combinations.

Related
-------

- clautolisp-boot-cwd-pwd-pathname-defaults.issue (cwd/PWD at boot —
  a special case of getting the base reference right).
- cad-path-dotdot-resolution.issue (`..` semantics once the base and
  rooting are correct).

Triage (clautolisp 1.2.10)
--------------------------

DEFERRED — kept open. This is a cross-environment design issue, not a
bounded bug: it requires a path-mapping layer driven by the
(build-env, run-env) pair, plus a conformance matrix across native
Windows / Cygwin / MSYS2 / WSL. None of that is reproducible or
testable on the Linux/macOS build hosts, so it cannot be implemented
and verified here.

The narrow, testable subset it shares with cadaptor's symptoms —
relative paths resolving against the live cwd at boot — was the
separable bug and is fixed in 1.2.8 (see
clautolisp-boot-cwd-pwd-pathname-defaults.issue). On macOS/Linux the
mapping is the identity, so that fix is sufficient there; the Windows
mapping layer remains future design work, to be scheduled against a
real Windows target.

A full design specification for the mapping layer (three frames of
reference, the mount-table model, map-in/map-out primitives, build- and
run-frame detection, edge-case rules, the conformance matrix, and a
W0..W5 implementation plan) is now written up in
clautolisp/documentation/clautolisp-windows-pathname-mapping-spec.org
(PDF beside it). This issue stays open as the tracking issue for that
spec.

Progress (2026-08-06)
---------------------

Branch feature-windows-pathname-mapping. The mapping layer is now
implemented; the whole thing short-circuits to the identity mapping on
macOS/Linux, so existing behaviour there is unchanged (runtime +
builtins-core + file-compat + tool suites all still green).

New code:
- clautolisp/autolisp-runtime/source/pathname-mapping.lisp — the
  package clautolisp.pathname-mapping (nickname clal-pathmap): the
  MAPPING-ENVIRONMENT / MOUNT-ENTRY model, *BUILD-ENVIRONMENT* /
  *RUN-ENVIRONMENT*, TO-CANONICAL / FROM-CANONICAL, MAP-IN / MAP-OUT
  (+ the string workhorses MAP-IN-NAMESTRING / MAP-OUT-NAMESTRING),
  CLASSIFY-ENVIRONMENT-KIND, the standard mount tables, build/run
  detection, and the UNMAPPABLE-PATH condition.
- clautolisp/autolisp-runtime/tests/pathname-mapping-tests.lisp —
  FiveAM tests (identity no-ops, WSL-trap classification, the synthetic
  cross-frame translation matrix, edge cases, round-trip stability).
- scripts/verify-pathname-mapping.lisp — the standalone §8 conformance
  harness (sbcl/ccl --load), runnable on any host; exercises the full
  synthetic (build x run) matrix plus a real-file round trip through the
  live frames. Meant for the [windows, amd64, shell] runner.

W-phase status:
- W0 (model + identity) .......... DONE. map-in/map-out + the two live
  environments; identity on :posix; §4.3 insertion points wired
  (resolve-open-pathname, resolve-existing-file -> map-in;
  builtin-findfile / builtin-findtrustedfile -> map-out; the boot cwd
  sync in tools/clautolisp main.lisp now also classifies the run frame).
- W1 (build-frame recording) ..... DONE. *BUILD-ENVIRONMENT* is captured
  at file-load time (so it is frozen into the dumped image) with a probe
  plist incl. (truename "/") per §5.1; BUILD-ENVIRONMENT accessor +
  DESCRIBE-MAPPING-ENVIRONMENTS; CLAUTOLISP_DEBUG_PATHMAP env var dumps
  both frames from the running binary (verified on the SBCL image).
- W2 (run-frame detection) ....... DONE. CLASSIFY-ENVIRONMENT-KIND
  (native / cygwin / msys2 / wsl / posix) is independent of
  uiop:os-windows-p so it does not fall into the WSL-reports-Linux trap
  (§1.1); INITIALIZE-RUN-ENVIRONMENT runs at tool startup.
- W3 (translation) ............... DONE (logic; needs Windows CI to
  confirm against real files). Non-identity map-in/map-out pivot through
  the canonical C:/ form; edge cases covered: mount-point translation,
  install-root remap (msys2 / -> C:/msys64/), ~ per run-frame home, UNC
  preservation, mixed separators, already-canonical input under a
  posix-rooted run frame, and the unmappable-path error.
- W4 (conformance matrix) ........ HARNESS DELIVERED
  (scripts/verify-pathname-mapping.lisp); GREEN on Linux. Needs the
  [windows, amd64, shell] CI job wired (job spec handed to the
  integrator) to run against real native/cygwin/msys2/wsl frames.
- W5 (optional logical pathnames)  TODO (out of scope; §7).

Still TODO after Windows CI confirms W3/W4: seed run-frame mount tables
from the live /etc/fstab (cygwin/msys2) and `mount` 9p/drvfs output
(WSL2) rather than the built-in defaults; today the standard tables use
the conventional /cygdrive, /<letter>, /mnt/<letter> prefixes and a
supplied install-root.

Progress (2026-08-15) — W4 CONFIRMED on real Windows, live mount tables
----------------------------------------------------------------------

W4 is done. The [windows, amd64, shell] job this issue was waiting for
turned out to be wired already (conformance:pathname-mapping:windows in
.gitlab/native.yml), and it now RUNS — the runner came back online and
the CAD tag fix let the native child pipeline through. Its output:

    ok    round-trip build=MSYS2 run=MSYS2 "/c/x/y/z.lsp"
    ok    round-trip build=CYGWIN run=NATIVE "C:/work/proj/a.lsp"
    ... (full synthetic build x run matrix)
    --- live-frame REAL FILE round trip (identity on Unix) ---
    ok    map-in of a real file resolves to an openable pathname
    ok    map-out(map-in(s)) round-trips to the run-frame form
    pathname-mapping conformance: OK (build=MSYS2 run=MSYS2)

Note the live frames: build=MSYS2 run=MSYS2, DETECTED — not a fallback to
:posix. So the classification of §5 works on the real machine, the whole
synthetic matrix passes there, and a real file round-trips through the
live frames. That is what W4 asked for.

W5 remains out of scope (§7, optional logical pathnames).

LIVE MOUNT TABLES — the issue's "Still TODO after Windows CI"

Implemented. STANDARD-MOUNT-TABLE described the CONVENTIONAL layout
(/cygdrive/c, /c, /mnt/c, a supplied install root), which is right on a
default install and wrong on any machine whose owner moved something: an
msys2 installed elsewhere than C:/msys64, an fstab mapping a share, a WSL
distro with extra drvfs mounts. The system already knows the truth —
cygwin and msys2 keep it in /etc/fstab, WSL reports it through `mount' —
and it is now read and PREFERRED, with the conventional table kept
underneath for whatever it does not mention (%MERGE-MOUNT-TABLES).

Parsing is separated from acquisition on purpose: PARSE-FSTAB-MOUNT-ENTRIES
and PARSE-MOUNT-COMMAND-ENTRIES are pure functions over text, so the
formats are tested on a host that has neither. LIVE-MOUNT-ENTRIES does the
reading and never signals — a machine with no /etc/fstab, no `mount', or
an unreadable one falls back to the conventions, because the mapping layer
must not fail to start because a probe did.

Details that needed care, each with a test:

- fstab's field order is (Windows path, POSIX mount point), i.e. exactly
  (canonical-prefix, frame-prefix);
- an fstab also lists pseudo-filesystems (none, cygdrive, proc) that name
  no physical Windows path — they are skipped rather than turned into a
  bogus mapping;
- fstab escapes a space as \040, which is what any path under
  "Program Files" needs, so it is decoded;
- under WSL both 9p (usually C:) and drvfs (the other drives) are Windows
  filesystems and both are kept; ext4/proc/tmpfs are the distro's own and
  are not.

The number of live entries found is recorded in the environment's probe
plist, so CLAUTOLISP_DEBUG_PATHMAP shows whether a frame was seeded from
the system or from the conventions.

Verified: SBCL and CCL suites green (exit 0), 7 new tests. The Windows
job exercises the result on the real machine.


CLOSED 2026-08-15 (clautolisp 1.8.38, merged as MR !81)
-------------------------------------------------------

Every work item this issue defined is delivered: W0 (model + identity),
W1 (build-frame recording), W2 (run-frame detection), W3 (translation)
and W4 (conformance matrix, now CONFIRMED on the real Windows runner
with build=MSYS2 run=MSYS2 detected rather than fallen back to :posix).

W5 is not left undone — it was declared out of scope by this issue's own
§7, which lists optional logical pathnames as a non-goal. Closing on the
scope the issue set for itself, not on a reduced one.

The "Still TODO after Windows CI" item (seed the mount tables from the
live system instead of the conventions) is delivered in the same release,
so nothing carries forward from this ticket. Should logical pathnames
ever be wanted, they warrant a new issue with their own justification —
reopening this one would resurrect a non-goal.
